{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2127 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3dab0a181c6ca65125fa5cf8c65cb2a222ac669e92aca9006387474ae5d3a541" :edb/db-before-t 2126 :edb/db-after-t 2127 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535439 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:05:25.931Z" 13194139535439 true] [17592186166787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349268 13194139535439 true] [17592186166787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349275 13194139535439 true] [17592186166788 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349289 13194139535439 true] [17592186166790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349282 13194139535439 true] [17592186167020 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535439 true] [17592186167020 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/660feebe44e179b53be85d23c497e58de4774258b588a6f8ac785a0fd9c8fc08.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$" 13194139535439 true] [17592186167020 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/q-116024.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{2y - y^{2} - 1}$\n2. $y^{′} =-\\frac{y^{2} + y}{y - 1}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{3}{y - 1}$" 13194139535439 false] [17592186167020 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/a76856ea0a6b50b821ba6c39e6a87a5acf5aabd704454b4875540b3970f3d249.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}=-\\frac{1}{(y-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for all $y\\ne 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}=-\\frac{y+1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to ∓\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>1.$" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349268 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349269 13194139535439 true] [17592186349268 1089 "q-115931" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349268 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535439 true] [17592186349268 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/4f9f16654bc92f01c9cda8308ab4679695ed7d5055a12f6963303d4071c95bea.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349268 1113 #uuid "cbebd003-2374-5d75-bbd7-db61c097cd65" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349268 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/0234179c0a2dfb514a921f19e6fdc7bcad022aa0368d122a7d2ecc0e5d27df73.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}(2-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(2-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y).$" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349270 13194139535439 true] [17592186349269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349271 13194139535439 true] [17592186349269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349272 13194139535439 true] [17592186349269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349273 13194139535439 true] [17592186349269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349274 13194139535439 true] [17592186349269 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535439 true] [17592186349269 1041 "selection" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349269 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349270 13194139535439 true] [17592186349269 1110 #uuid "5d060ea3-1e32-50c4-bdf4-7688d9fcc126" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349270 1034 "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349270 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349270 1111 #uuid "c8880916-6af5-578d-965e-fd9d6f88b4a4" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349271 1034 "{y}^{′}=y(2-y)" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349271 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349271 1111 #uuid "4464d7b8-9772-5def-8606-4ad8529a8072" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349272 1034 "{y}^{′}=-y(y+2)" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349272 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349272 1111 #uuid "d8be0ffd-3951-5730-88fb-afe685a3ecdc" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349273 1034 "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+2)" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349273 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349273 1111 #uuid "58255768-8bc8-58d7-9589-322253ac8e5a" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349274 1034 "{y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349274 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349274 1111 #uuid "0e4b6a89-ec92-5a01-8642-f1e512c8a05f" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349275 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349276 13194139535439 true] [17592186349275 1089 "q-120150" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349275 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535439 true] [17592186349275 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/8d2588ba2b8987841a34e981aa6b7d48cdc41bebfca6c0517cc9cf67d788f591.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349275 1113 #uuid "10795815-c959-55ee-95fa-9d0fe7de376e" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349275 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1b5303303f0aea22149b3d418e08724cfea9cf64943eab978fe38c0d025d73bf.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-1)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-1) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)^{2}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}.$" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349277 13194139535439 true] [17592186349276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349278 13194139535439 true] [17592186349276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349279 13194139535439 true] [17592186349276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349280 13194139535439 true] [17592186349276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349281 13194139535439 true] [17592186349276 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535439 true] [17592186349276 1041 "selection" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349276 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349281 13194139535439 true] [17592186349276 1110 #uuid "21608e77-ff9a-5f24-b33b-b56b7a3ed722" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349277 1034 "{y}^{′}=y-1" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349277 1111 #uuid "6ac38a65-a187-54e3-92d9-1a95c96d36b3" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349278 1034 "{y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349278 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349278 1111 #uuid "b4b8df4f-64f4-53c5-a1d5-824be7db6b55" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349279 1034 "{y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)^{2}" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349279 1111 #uuid "74f6150b-ebb6-59fa-aa62-0bd74bae70a0" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349280 1034 "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349280 1111 #uuid "a94018d1-7bba-5224-be71-d6c40a0ec287" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349281 1034 "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349281 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349281 1111 #uuid "1bb147d5-bd44-5f2a-ac96-8461c8c1b811" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349282 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349283 13194139535439 true] [17592186349282 1089 "q-120174" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349282 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535439 true] [17592186349282 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/e0da4ce40e23a44ddfd946b9a9bf00882ca0a83db8379c6492b58355772c6006.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349282 1113 #uuid "9b5e100b-a820-51ba-91b9-bf3f389b5600" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349282 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/ddac2bab6fb72979e1142179dfb11792cc50044de545131139edfdeff9215e3c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=\\pm 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4=(y+2)(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}.$" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349284 13194139535439 true] [17592186349283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349285 13194139535439 true] [17592186349283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349286 13194139535439 true] [17592186349283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349287 13194139535439 true] [17592186349283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349288 13194139535439 true] [17592186349283 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535439 true] [17592186349283 1041 "selection" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349283 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349286 13194139535439 true] [17592186349283 1110 #uuid "09d21b21-7972-54f5-8395-7a49ffd9202d" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349284 1034 "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349284 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349284 1111 #uuid "e030a874-8cc6-5792-93dd-b62236dccf6c" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349285 1034 "{y}^{′}=4y-{y}^{3}" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349285 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349285 1111 #uuid "9cef04ac-7a45-5764-8739-ed73f42f48fb" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349286 1034 "{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349286 1111 #uuid "b7a0547a-86f5-5a90-b1a8-205d0090b09e" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349287 1034 "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349287 1111 #uuid "e7984484-a896-5166-b786-0ae945f1eb11" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349288 1034 "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-4y" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349288 1111 #uuid "f5351c0e-1abd-5fa8-ad15-532159bd4109" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349289 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349290 13194139535439 true] [17592186349289 1089 "q-120196" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349289 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535439 true] [17592186349289 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1a3ba94aa37bd8b8494794a7013956b100f418c9e0aa7efecab5ba6fc6476ff6.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139535439 true] [17592186349289 1113 #uuid "c80401aa-a1e2-5ade-89cc-25d35cd9468c" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349289 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1a3ba94aa37bd8b8494794a7013956b100f418c9e0aa7efecab5ba6fc6476ff6.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(y+1)(y-1)} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(1+y)(1-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349291 13194139535439 true] [17592186349290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349292 13194139535439 true] [17592186349290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349293 13194139535439 true] [17592186349290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349294 13194139535439 true] [17592186349290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349295 13194139535439 true] [17592186349290 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535439 true] [17592186349290 1041 "selection" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349290 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349293 13194139535439 true] [17592186349290 1110 #uuid "1031247c-3ada-554e-8663-53ce0189cb7f" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349291 1034 "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}+1" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535439 true] [17592186349291 1111 #uuid "454c75b7-c5f5-57e8-932c-689b569fe715" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349292 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y-1}{{y}^{2}+1}" 13194139535439 true] [17592186349292 1